! Silicon, 2 atoms in the primitive fcc cell, PAW, one k-point.
!
! The timing counterpart of tests/data/qe/si2-paw.in, cut to a single
! k-point for the same reason every other benchmark here is: both codes
! parallelise over k, so a multi-k comparison measures batching rather than the
! cost of the physics.
!
! An PAW run is a different shape of calculation from a norm-conserving one,
! not just a bigger one. The density lives on a grid with a dual of 8 while the
! wavefunctions live on one with a dual of 4, so the FFTs come in two sizes; the
! augmentation charge has to be built on the larger grid every time the density
! is; and the nonlocal coefficients are rebuilt from the potential every
! iteration instead of being read once from a file. Where the time goes is
! therefore worth measuring separately, not extrapolating from si-1k.in.
 &control
    calculation = 'scf'
 /
 &system
    ibrav=2, celldm(1)=10.20, nat=2, ntyp=1,
    ecutwfc=20.0, ecutrho=160.0
 /
 &electrons
    conv_thr = 1.0d-10
 /
ATOMIC_SPECIES
 Si  28.086  Si.pz-n-kjpaw_psl.0.1.UPF
ATOMIC_POSITIONS (alat)
 Si 0.00 0.00 0.00
 Si 0.25 0.25 0.25
K_POINTS
  1
   0.250000  0.250000  0.250000   1.00
